)}(1)}{5!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(1)}{5!} & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(5)}(1)}{120} & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(1) & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413290 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413290 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413290 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413290 #edb/ref 1071 "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ about $x=0$ is" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413290 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca3460ff-fc6a-5037-a239-c26e4d66f531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413290 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!} \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(0) & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =0+2\\cdot x+0\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+(-8)\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27bb1f1e-f0b6-55ad-af80-59be0e465969" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186413290 #edb/ref 1759